      "math_academy_id": "q-120279",
      "knowledge_point_id": "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/fc7d37da99173b7fa99f2f5f0bbf1478a54505628ed827488a18b3d01535f81f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073957/assets/6c9273c0b89d4c06c8a8591b8e8e94b0c83b2d1d4bd04b64ed8cb0a185619b5f.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OJ=|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ has the opposite direction to $j$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-j.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $JL=2|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ has the opposite direction to $k$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2k.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l & =\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL} \\\\  & =-j-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-j-2k.$\n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$",
